Z||000000||speed racer||10-28-2001||12:29 AM||||Dose any one knows how to paint the outer edge of the rotor where the brake pads doesn’t touch it. I have new 5-star wheels and you can see them I have tried just hi-temp spray paint and it didn’t hold up very long at all.<p>[This message has been edited by speed racer (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||watts||10-28-2001||12:43 AM||||VHT makes drum/rotor paint.  Higher temp rating than normal engine paint, and is supposed to stand up to the usual chemicals one would encounter "down there".<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||speed racer||10-28-2001||01:32 AM||||How would I keep form getting the paint on the part of the rotor that the brake pads rub on.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DR650SE||10-28-2001||01:35 AM||||I don't think it would matter if paint got on the rotor because the pads would rub the paint off.  Bet a second opinion.<P>------------------<BR>2 Black 86 SE's V-6 4-SPD||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||10-28-2001||02:01 AM||||If you get a lot of paint on the rotor face, you're liable to have to replace your pads.  They'll gum up with paint, I'd imagine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||10-28-2001||02:23 AM||||Solvent base, BAD...<BR>Masking, GOOD<BR>Pull the things off, tape to hell the used portion of the rotors, paint, remove tape, cure, run emery cloth over area JUST to be sure...<P>BTW, I'm fairly sure that brake cleaner WILL eat through the paint next time you clean 'em<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000006||rogergarrison||10-28-2001||05:29 AM||||ya, the paint will gum up the pads. You can just paint the edges with a small 1/2" brush||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||10-28-2001||08:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by speed racer:<BR><B>I have tried just hi-temp spray paint and it didn’t hold up very long at all.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Use whatever method you did before, just use the drum/rotor paint....<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||watts||10-28-2001||01:02 PM||||When I used to work at a garage, we had a guy come in who's rotor's needed turning.  He knew this, so he painted them around the outer edges, and the hubs THEN brought them to us.  Of course, after we machined them, there was a perfect edge where we had 'cut the paint off'.<P>Something to consider if yours are a hair off of true.<BR>||||reg||1
